invariant: INV-16
file: p256_wide_mul.c
catches: proof/prove-one.sh p256_wide_digit
reason: the bit read drops its test of the position against 255, so the
  top six-bit window reads bits 256 and 257, which index the scalar's
  limb 8 of 8: a read past the object. The bits it reads land above the
  top digit's terms, so the digits can still come out right and a vector
  need not notice. proof/p256_wide_digit_harness.c proves every limb
  index in bounds for every window at both widths. Each run is a proof,
  so this belongs to the nightly's test-invariants-proof-backed job.

--- old
    if (i > 255) {
        return 0;
    }
    return scalar_bit(k, i);
--- new
    return scalar_bit(k, i);
